catcat> \ ======================================================================
\ 08 — declaring a type, and taking it apart.
\
\ THE CLAIM (D-89..D-93): a sum type needs no new machinery. A constructor is
\ `PInj` at a tag, which the core already had; a `case` is a HANDLER whose
\ implementations are the branches and whose body is a single `dispatch`,
\ which the core already had too (D-68). So `data` and `case` are surface
\ syntax over `M05` as it stood — no new core term, no new typing rule.
\
\ The honest gap is at the bottom: declared types are STRUCTURAL, not nominal.
\ ======================================================================
catcat> \ --- 1. An enumeration. Every variant carries nothing.
catcat> data Color { alt Red ( ) alt Green ( ) alt Blue ( ) }
data Color {
  alt Red ( )
  alt Green ( )
  alt Blue ( )
}
catcat> \ The constructors are not words and live in their own namespace (D-91) —
\ `locate` reports what a name constructs and at which tag.
catcat> locate Green
Green constructs Color at tag 1, carrying ( )

data Color {
  alt Red ( )
  alt Green ( )
  alt Blue ( )
}
catcat> \ --- 2. `case` selects on the constructor NAME, so branch order is free and
\ reordering the declaration cannot silently change what this means.
catcat> define channel ( Color -- i64 ) { case { Blue { 3 } Red { 1 } Green { 2 } } }
defined channel ( sum[ ( ) ( ) ( ) ] -- i64 )
catcat> Red channel show print " " print
Green channel show print " " print
Blue channel show print "\n" print
1 2 3
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- 3. A missing branch is an error, and it names what is missing.
catcat> define partial ( Color -- i64 ) { case { Red { 1 } } }
error: this case is not exhaustive; nothing selects Green Blue. Add a branch for each, or an 'else'
catcat> \ --- 4. `else` covers the rest. ONE implementation is shared by every variant
\ it covers (D-92), not one copy each.
catcat> define is_red ( Color -- i64 ) { case { Red { 1 } else { 0 } } }
defined is_red ( sum[ ( ) ( ) ( ) ] -- i64 )
catcat> Red is_red show print " " print
Blue is_red show print "\n" print
1 0
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- 5. Variants may carry values. A branch enters with its payload already
\ on the stack.
catcat> data Shape { alt Circle ( i64 ) alt Rect ( i64 i64 ) alt Point ( ) }
data Shape {
  alt Circle ( i64 )
  alt Rect ( i64 i64 )
  alt Point ( )
}
catcat> define area ( Shape -- i64 ) { case { Circle { dup * 3 * } Rect { * } Point { 0 } } }
defined area ( sum[ ( i64 ) ( i64 i64 ) ( ) ] -- i64 )
catcat> 5 Circle area show print " " print
3 4 Rect area show print " " print
Point area show print "\n" print
75 12 0
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- 6. Which is why an `else` cannot cover variants that carry DIFFERENT
\ things: the block runs with the payload pushed, so a different payload is a
\ different entry stack. One block cannot have two.
catcat> define guess ( Shape -- i64 ) { case { Point { 0 } else { pop 1 } } }
error: the variants this 'else' covers carry different values, so one block cannot run for all of them; write a branch for each
catcat> \ --- 7. The residual. A `case` IS a handler (D-68) — three implementations
\ and a dispatch — and this is the whole of it, not a rendering.
catcat> locate area
define area ( sum[ ( i64 ) ( i64 i64 ) ( ) ] -- i64 ) {
  handle !5 over (  ) init { } { #147 { dup * 3 * } #148 { * } #149 { 0 } } { dispatch #147 #148 #149 }
}
catcat> \ --- 8. Type parameters, from the start. `Some` binds `#T` by matching what it
\ carries against the stack; `None` carries nothing, so it cannot, and says so.
catcat> data Option[#T] { alt None ( ) alt Some ( #T ) }
data Option[#T] {
  alt None ( )
  alt Some ( #T )
}
catcat> 42 Some
ok  #1(42)
catcat> None
error: None: #T is not determined by what it carries; write the types at the call site, as in None[i64]
catcat> pop
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- 9. A generic word over a parameterised type, called implicitly at two
\ different types. `#T` is fixed by the bare `#T` input, which is reached
\ first; `Option[#T]` is ground by the time matching gets to it (D-93).
catcat> define orelse[#T] ( Option[#T] #T -- #T ) { swap case { Some { swap pop } None { } } }
generic orelse[#T]
catcat> 42 Some 0 orelse show print " " print
None[i64] 7 orelse show print "\n" print
42 7
ok  (empty)
catcat> "hello" Some "?" orelse print "\n" print
hello
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ --- 10. THE GAP. A declared type is its representation and nothing more, so
\ two declarations with the same variant shapes are the same type — and a
\ `case` written for one typechecks over the other. Signatures print the shape
\ rather than a name for exactly this reason: there is no name to recover.
catcat> data Traffic { alt Stop ( ) alt Slow ( ) alt Go ( ) }
data Traffic {
  alt Stop ( )
  alt Slow ( )
  alt Go ( )
}
catcat> Stop channel show print "\n" print
1
ok  (empty)
catcat> \ Fixing it needs a nominal declaration — `M01.TName` and a `nom_id` table —
\ which is N02 Q-13, and is not built.
